Rescoped for main (release 1.000 rewrite). t/basic.t covers collect_changes, generate_diff and apply_changes on a temp tree. Not covered:
- run(): the positional CLI - no argument (diff with fork.conf), a config argument, apply [conf], output [conf] file, output without a file (dies), a missing config (dies). run() reads fork.conf relative to the cwd and works on the cwd, so the test has to chdir into its own temp dir (and back) - never run it in the repository.
- config parsing: empty lines and lines without | or % are ignored; a|b|c splits into from a and to b|c (limit 2); a line with both separators is a replace rule.
- the name rule takes every replace rule in order, each sees the output of the previous one (the codefork-usage skill documents this ordering).
Done when each of these has an assertion in t/ and prove -lr t/ is green.

2026-10-21 20:24 Also found (candidates for new cards, this board): (1) the default fork.conf lives in the tree it rewrites - 'Old|New' + 'codefork apply' turns fork.conf into 'New|New' (and a config named Old.conf would be renamed); (2) every rewritten file loses its mode - spew_raw writes a fresh file, so executable bits (bin/ scripts) drop to 0664, reproduced with chmod 755 Old.sh; (3) a file path whose new parent exists as a *file* (Old/a.txt -> New/a.txt with a file New) passes the k10 check and dies mid-apply in mkpath after earlier changes were written; (4) dzil test only gathers git-tracked tests, so the six new t/*.t join the release-path run only once committed.

